            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "4 tr-mesh repeats x 8 rows = 10 x 10 cm after blocking. Gauge varies significantly with yarn; check after blocking a swatch."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pattern notes"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Treble mesh row (right side): ch 5 (counts as tr, ch 2), tr in next tr, ch 2; at spine work (tr, ch 2, tr) in centre space; continue. Wrong-side row: ch 5, tr in each tr with ch-2 between, working plain over the spine (tr, ch 2, tr)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Foundation"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chain 10. Row 1 (right side): tr in 6th ch, ch 2, (tr, ch 2, tr, ch 2, tr) in last ch (spine). Turn. (5 tr)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Body"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Right-side rows: ch 5, "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "treble-mesh-l9"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "treble mesh"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " across left half, (tr, ch 2, tr) in spine sp, mesh across right half, tr in t-ch. Turn."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Wrong-side rows: ch 5, mesh across to end. Turn."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Increase rate: 4 tr added per right-side row (2 per half). Work to approx. 60 cm depth (about 48 right-side rows) or yarn is nearly used."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Border"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Work 1 round of dc along each edge with 3 dc at each corner. Work 3 dc into each ch-2 sp along the curved lower edge to keep it from pulling. Join with slip stitch. Fasten off."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in ends. Wet the shawl. Use blocking wires along the two straight edges and pin the lower scalloped edge with individual pins. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "blocking-l9"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "Blocking"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is essential for lace weight; the unblocked fabric looks far smaller than the finished shawl. Leave flat until fully dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to crochet next"
